              "text": "{\n  \"query_text\": \"What is the WHO recommended daily sodium intake limit for adults to reduce cardiovascular risks, and what specific demographic groups and health conditions are excluded from the conditional recommendation to use potassium-containing lower-sodium salt substitutes?\",\n  \"target_population\": [\n    \"adults\",\n    \"pregnant women\",\n    \"children\"\n  ],\n  \"conditions\": [\n    \"kidney impairments\",\n    \"conditions compromising potassium excretion\",\n    \"cardiovascular disease\",\n    \"stroke\",\n    \"coronary heart disease\"\n  ],\n  \"draft_rationale\": \"To fully answer the query, information must be merged from both passages. The 2012 guideline establishes the baseline strong recommendation for adults to reduce sodium intake to <2 g/day (5 g/day salt) to reduce blood pressure and risks of cardiovascular disease, stroke, and coronary heart disease. The 2025 guideline introduces the conditional recommendation to replace regular table salt with potassium-containing lower-sodium salt substitutes (LSSS) to align with this sodium target, while specifying the demographic exclusions (pregnant women and children) and health condition exclusions (individuals with kidney impairments or other conditions compromising potassium excretion).\"\n}",
